#{[17592186247248 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{x}}=\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}3\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}8\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=3\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+8\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{1}=3.$"] [17592186247249 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\},$ where \n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$"] [17592186247249 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{x}}=\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}4\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}0\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}5\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=4\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+0\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{2}+5\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}=4.$"] [17592186247250 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$"] [17592186247250 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{x}}=\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}2\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}3\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}6\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=2\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+3\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{2}+6\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}=3.$"] [17592186247252 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 41\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [17592186247252 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 41\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 41\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 41\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -13 & 26\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ -13{x}_{2} &=26.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(-2)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-9)+(-2)=-11.$"] [17592186247255 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$"] [17592186247255 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -{x}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom third equation we get ${x}_{3}=-1$ and substituting this value into the second equation yields ${x}_{2}=-2(-1)+3=5.$ Finally, substituting ${x}_{2}=5$ and ${x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(5)+2(-1)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-6)+5+(-1)=-2.\n$$"] [17592186247256 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$"] [17592186247256 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -8 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 11 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -8 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 11 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -8 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -8 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{7}\\right){R}_{3} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 21\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=11 \\\\ -7{x}_{3} &=-14 \\\\ 0 &=21,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere the fourth equation is always false. Therefore, the system has no solution and we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186247257 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$"] [17592186247257 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -4 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -4 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -4 & -22\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -16\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=7 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ 8{x}_{3} &=-16.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom third equation we get ${x}_{3}=-2$ and substituting this value into the second equation yields ${x}_{2}=-2(-2)+1=5.$ Finally, substituting ${x}_{2}=5$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+5=7\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+5+(-2)=5.\n$$"] [17592186319175 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [17592186319175 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 22 & 22 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{22}{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 22 & 22 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ 22{x}_{2} &=22.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+4(1)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2+1=-1.$"] [17592186319176 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [17592186319176 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 8{x}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$"] [17592186319177 :question/problem "Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$, if $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ and \n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186319177 :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathcal{B}.$ Since we are told that $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & =\\underbrace{(-1)}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}5\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ 40 \\\\ -45\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 44 \\\\ -48\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186319186 :question/problem "Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$, if $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ and \n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186319186 :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathcal{B}.$ Since we are told that $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & =\\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ -30 \\\\ -54\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -34 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320959 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$"] [17592186320959 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}3 & 5 & 0 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 10 & -2 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}3 & 5 & 0 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 10 & -2 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}3 & 5 & 0 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}3 & 5 & 0 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=10 \\\\ 5{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ -2{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ 0 &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the fourth equation, we get a true statement. Then, from the second and third equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1$ and  ${x}_{3}=2$ respectively. Substituting ${x}_{2}=1$ into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}+5(1)=10\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}.$"] [17592186345146 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$"] [17592186345146 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{x}}=\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}-6\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}4\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=(-6)\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+4\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{1}=-6.$"] [17592186345147 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$"] [17592186345147 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{x}}=\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}4\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}-3\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=4\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+(-3)\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-3.$"] [17592186345150 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$"] [17592186345150 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{x}}=\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}-8\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}4\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}2\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=-8\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+4\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{2}+2\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}=4.$"] [17592186345152 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{3}?$"]}
